<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Since 1 May 2024, an SDK using a required-reason API must declare it in a privacy
  manifest or App Store Connect rejects the upload with ITMS-91053 (Missing API
  declaration). That rejection lands on the merchant's upload, not ours: shipping this
  file wrong or absent breaks every integrator's release.

  Known failure mode to validate before release: AppsFlyer's manifest failed to bundle
  correctly in the static SPM variant (their issue #281). Propagation must be checked
  against a real consumer app, not just a local `swift build`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      No tracking as defined by App Tracking Transparency. Decided, not deferred: nothing
      in the SDK path joins data with another company's advertising data and nothing
      reaches a data broker. The affiliate integration that would have complicated the
      answer lives in the Frak wallet app, not in this SDK.

      Cross-merchant linkage does exist — one wallet accumulates rewards from several
      merchants — but only once the user authenticates with that wallet, and it serves
      reward distribution, not advertising measurement.

      If an ad network ever enters the SDK path, this key, NSPrivacyTrackingDomains and
      the NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking flags below all change together.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array>
        <!--
          Covers two identifiers: the anonymous id, and the `customerId` a merchant hands
          to `trackPurchase(customerId:orderId:token:)`.

          The anonymous id is NOT a Device ID, whatever its name suggests. Apple's Device
          ID is "the device's advertising identifier, or other device-level ID"; this one
          is per app installation, unreadable by another app, and gone on uninstall
          (`DeviceKey.swift` refuses Keychain so it cannot survive one). It is Apple's
          "assigned user ID ... that can be used to identify a particular user or account".

          Linked: the install handoff exists to let the wallet join this id to the user's
          Frak identity, so unlinked would be false the moment `openFrakApp()` succeeds.

          AppFunctionality only — the id routes attribution and rewards. Merchant-facing
          reporting is derived from Product Interaction below, which carries Analytics.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!--
          `trackPurchase(customerId:orderId:token:)` transmits the merchant's own
          customer and order identifiers plus a checkout token, so a payout can be
          reconciled. That is purchase history under Apple's taxonomy even though no
          amount or line item is sent. Nothing in this array is an upload gate — that is
          NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes below — so accuracy here is about describing the
          merchant's app correctly, not about passing ingestion.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!--
          `track(_:)` posts `arrival`, `sharing` and `custom` interactions to
          /user/track/interaction. In-app interaction events are exactly Apple's
          "Product Interaction", and this is the SDK's headline feature.

          `Interaction.custom(_:data:)` carries a merchant-supplied [String: String].
          The SDK cannot declare what a merchant chooses to put in it: that payload is
          the merchant's own declaration responsibility, and sdk/ios/README.md says so.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
    <!--
      UserDefaults is one of the five required-reason categories. This SDK keeps the
      resolved merchant config (suite `id.frak.sdk.config`) and the tracking-consent
      decision (suite `id.frak.sdk.consent`) there. The anonymous id is not: it lives in
      `FileKeyValueStore` (Application Support/id.frak.sdk/identity.json), an ordinary
      file whose timestamps are never read — as is the event queue. Neither WebKit nor
      UIPasteboard is in a category.

      CA92.1 is the right code: it covers a third-party SDK reading or writing
      UserDefaults in the app's own container, on the app's behalf, which is what this
      SDK does. C56D.1 is for an SDK that exposes a UserDefaults wrapper API for the
      app itself to call. This SDK exposes no such API, so C56D.1 would be the wrong
      declaration.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
